# What NOT to ship to Fly's builder.
#
# `flyctl deploy .` tars the working tree and uploads it. The Dockerfile does
# not COPY .env, but the tarball still carries it — the secret transits
# regardless of whether a layer ever references it. Same class of mistake as
# passing a key in argv where `ps` can read it: the leak is in the transport,
# not the destination.
#
# Deny-by-default would be safer still, but the build genuinely needs
# pyproject.toml, README.md, src/ and fly/, and an allowlist that drifts out of
# sync with the Dockerfile fails closed in a confusing way. So: an explicit
# deny list, with the secret-bearing entries first.

# --- secrets and local credentials (the reason this file exists) -----------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key

# --- local state that is never part of a build -----------------------------
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# --- vcs, tooling, and things with their own build ------------------------
.git/
.github/
.gitignore
vendor/
dashboard/
skills/
assets/
# The retired desktop app (D17): no image builds from it, and its Rust build
# output and node_modules made every `just door-deploy` upload 7.3 GB.
app/
# At any depth: a bare `node_modules/` only matches the one at the root.
**/node_modules/
